1
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,560
OK.

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,280
Let's get started.

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:09,840
So welcome back, everyone.

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,320
So today we're going to talk about parallelism.

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:15,320
And remember in the last week, we

6
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:20,200
introduced how to make a single GPU go fast by writing kernels.

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,200
And we really looked inside this GPU.

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,000
And this week, we're going to talk

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,720
about how to leverage multiple GPUs to make

10
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:30,888
your code go even faster.

11
00:00:30,888 --> 00:00:32,680
So the picture you should have in your head

12
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:34,400
is something like this.

13
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,760
So for the last week, we focused on one of these boxes,

14
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:40,080
where you have your GPU.

15
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:44,100
Remember, you have your High Bandwidth Memory, HBM, L2 cache,

16
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:51,680
L1 cache, registers, and a bunch of streaming multiprocessors.

17
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,640
So now the picture gets extended,

18
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:56,860
because instead of having one GPU, you might have four,

19
00:00:56,860 --> 00:00:59,260
you might have 1,000 GPUs.

20
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:01,120
And those are going to be connected.

21
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:04,080
And I'll talk later about how those GPUs are

22
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:06,048
going to get connected.

23
00:01:06,048 --> 00:01:07,840
And then you're going to have to figure out

24
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,840
how to leverage all of this compute to train models.

25
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:16,880
So in both cases, meaning both in the single GPU case

26
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:20,280
and in the multi-GPU case, as we'll talk about today,

27
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,260
the situation is kind of similar if you zoom out,

28
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:27,660
which is that the compute, the arithmetic logic units,

29
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:31,080
the tensor core, and so on, is far away from your data.

30
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:33,400
And far away for a single GPU means all the way

31
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,760
over here in HBM.

32
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:39,920
And now if you have multi GPUs, the thing that you need here

33
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:42,080
might be all the way on a different GPU,

34
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,200
and you're going to have to shuffle that over somehow.

35
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:46,880
But the same principles are going

36
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:49,520
to be the same, because the game is

37
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:54,320
to orchestrate the computation to try to avoid data transfer

38
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:55,280
bottlenecks.

39
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,880
It's very easy to use a ton of GPUs,

40
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:01,350
but it's hard to use them effectively.

41
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:04,090
So taking a little bit of liberty,

42
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:06,710
we can think about the generalized hierarchy

43
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:13,750
where at the local level next to the SMs

44
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:18,190
a single node single GPU where you have an L1 cache

45
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:18,970
shared memory.

46
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:20,470
This was the fastest.

47
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:25,910
And then you had HBM, which we lamented was so slow.

48
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:30,590
But in this lecture, HBM is going to be considered fast.

49
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:33,870
Now we're going to think about the single node multi-GPU

50
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:36,390
setting where the GPUs are going to be connected

51
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:38,950
via NVLink and NVSwitch.

52
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:42,370
And then finally, the multi-node and multi-GPU,

53
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:49,370
where we have to resort to InfiniBand ethernet depending on what network you have.

54
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:54,790
So last week, we talked about various tricks

55
00:02:54,790 --> 00:03:00,270
for reducing memory accesses, fusion and tiling.

56
00:03:00,270 --> 00:03:03,830
Read into shared memory, do as much as you can,

57
00:03:03,830 --> 00:03:05,990
and then write it back out.

58
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:07,590
And this week, we're going to talk

59
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:10,830
about how you can reduce the amount of communication

60
00:03:10,830 --> 00:03:17,470
across GPUs by replicating and sharding appropriately.

61
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:21,230
So why do you do multi-GPUs?

62
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:23,910
The obvious answer is, well, you want to scale.

63
00:03:23,910 --> 00:03:28,670
But to put a finer point on it, there's really two reasons.

64
00:03:28,670 --> 00:03:32,990
One is that your parameters or activations or gradients

65
00:03:32,990 --> 00:03:37,890
and optimizer state don't fit on the HBM memory of a single GPU.

66
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:40,670
So B21 has 192 gigabytes.

67
00:03:40,670 --> 00:03:42,930
If you're training a 1 trillion parameter model,

68
00:03:42,930 --> 00:03:46,070
that's not going to fit on a single GPU.

69
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:49,070
And the other reason is that even if your model could

70
00:03:49,070 --> 00:03:51,150
fit on a single GPU, you might want

71
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:54,630
to leverage more GPUs by splitting everything up

72
00:03:54,630 --> 00:03:56,510
to train faster.

73
00:03:56,510 --> 00:03:58,990
So sometimes there will be some decisions

74
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:02,930
to be made because you could fit everything on GPUs,

75
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:06,190
but you have fewer cores.

76
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:08,230
But if you spread it out, then you're

77
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:11,550
going to have to pay the communication bandwidth.

78
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:13,110
So that's some calculation you're

79
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:18,470
going to have to do to figure out how to parallelize.

80
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:24,490
So just one note here is that so far this lecture is Python.

81
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:27,350
You execute it and you can just show everything.

82
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:30,550
Now, in this lecture, if you run this directly,

83
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:33,630
it uses multiprocessing.

84
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:36,070
But when I trace through it, I'm putting it in some

85
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:38,647
special single process mode.

86
00:04:38,647 --> 00:04:40,230
So if you want to see the standard out

87
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:45,070
for this lecture run in the multiprocessing setup,

88
00:04:45,070 --> 00:04:46,050
you can click here.

89
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:48,750
And I'll show this as we go through the lecture.

90
00:04:48,750 --> 00:04:51,090
But just remember as we step through this lecture,

91
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,150
we're not actually doing multiprocessing,

92
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:56,990
because we're just stepping through single lines of code.

93
00:04:56,990 --> 00:05:01,910
So this lecture is going to include two parts.

94
00:05:01,910 --> 00:05:04,430
One is we're going to learn about the building

95
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:08,510
blocks of distributed communication and computation,

96
00:05:08,510 --> 00:05:10,930
starting with the programming model.

97
00:05:10,930 --> 00:05:13,430
Talk a little bit about the hardware.

98
00:05:13,430 --> 00:05:16,070
Start to implement things in Torch,

99
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:20,790
which you're going to do on your assignment two.

100
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:22,750
And then the second part is we're going

101
00:05:22,750 --> 00:05:25,470
to look at actual training.

102
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:28,430
We're going to look at three types of parallelism.

103
00:05:28,430 --> 00:05:31,470
Data parallelism, tensor parallelism, pipeline parallelism.

104
00:05:31,470 --> 00:05:34,530
Each is going to cut up our model in different ways.

105
00:05:34,530 --> 00:05:38,230
We're going to do this for MLPs rather than

106
00:05:38,230 --> 00:05:39,490
the full transformer.

107
00:05:39,490 --> 00:05:42,870
But it's really the core computation

108
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:45,630
is going to be shown here.

109
00:05:45,630 --> 00:05:50,390
So let's dive in.

110
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:53,540
So the first thing to talk about is these things

111
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:56,380
called collective operations.

112
00:05:56,380 --> 00:05:59,620
So collective operations are these primitives from

113
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:03,200
distributed programming that go back to the '80s.

114
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:07,460
So the idea of parallel programming is very old.

115
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:10,820
It wasn't invented for LLM training.

116
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:14,180
And it's still the case that these primitives

117
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:18,140
are the ones that we use today.

118
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:20,140
And here, collective just means that you're

119
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:22,260
specifying a general communication

120
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:25,560
pattern or a template across multiple devices,

121
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:28,660
rather than managing point-to-point how

122
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:32,020
this GPU is going to communicate with another GPU.

123
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:34,360
And this is going to be much easier,

124
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:36,720
and the system can do a lot more work for you.

125
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:40,740
So this is a very tried and true interface

126
00:06:40,740 --> 00:06:44,140
for doing parallel programming.

127
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:47,580
So the general setup is as follows.

128
00:06:47,580 --> 00:06:49,540
The terminology here is a little bit--

129
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:51,860
I find it a little bit strange, but this is standard

130
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:55,100
in parallel programming.

131
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:58,740
The idea is that you have a bunch of ranks

132
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:02,020
where rank corresponds to a particular device, in our case

133
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:02,640
a GPU.

134
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:03,980
It could be a TPU.

135
00:07:03,980 --> 00:07:06,780
But the point is that you have, let's say, four ranks here.

136
00:07:06,780 --> 00:07:09,960
The world size corresponds to the number of devices,

137
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:13,060
so the world size here is four.

138
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:17,620
So there are a few operations we're going to go through.

139
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:19,940
Broadcast, scatter, gather, reduce,

140
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:23,940
all gather, reduce scatter, all reduce, and all to all.

141
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:25,820
And each of these operations is going

142
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:29,780
to specify how this set of ranks or devices

143
00:07:29,780 --> 00:07:33,020
is going to transfer some amount of data

144
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:37,220
slash compute with it to some other set of devices.

145
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:40,660
So the first four, broadcast, scatter, gather, reduce,

146
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:44,380
are really just warm-ups, I would say.

147
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:51,540
They allow you to get a sense of how these collective operations

148
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:53,020
work, but they're not really going

149
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:56,100
to be the ones that are driving most of training.

150
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:57,700
All gather, reduce scatter, and all

151
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:59,620
reduce are the ones that are going

152
00:07:59,620 --> 00:08:04,180
to show up again and again for distributed training of language

153
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:05,080
models.

154
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:06,460
And finally, all to all I'll just

155
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:11,280
mention here, which is important for MoEs,

156
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:13,700
but we're not going to actually spend too much time

157
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:16,060
on this in this lecture.

158
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:21,780
OK, so let's dive in with the simplest operation, which

159
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:23,340
is broadcast.

160
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:26,920
So in broadcasting, you have a rank 0.

161
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:30,660
It could be any rank, but let's just say, for the sake of picking

162
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:36,380
one, rank 0, has some tensor 0123.

163
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:39,179
And it broadcasts it to all the ranks.

164
00:08:39,179 --> 00:08:44,420
So at the end of this operation, we have that each of the ranks

165
00:08:44,420 --> 00:08:48,100
has the same tensor on it.

166
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:51,580
That should be pretty straightforward.

167
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:54,300
And this, again, doesn't really show up

168
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:57,100
in the core path of training.

169
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:01,200
Generally, broadcasts are used for initialization,

170
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:04,540
where, let's say, you initialize a load, initial checkpoint

171
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:07,140
and then you broadcast it to all the ranks.

172
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:10,260
So something that's done like once.

173
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:15,380
So the second operation is a scatter.

174
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:23,340
And a scatter basically says, I have a tensor at rank 0

175
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:27,260
who is split up into the world size.

176
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,900
And I'm going to basically scatter

177
00:09:29,900 --> 00:09:32,860
my tensor onto the other ranks.

178
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:36,780
So rank 0 gets 0, the zeroth component.

179
00:09:36,780 --> 00:09:41,740
Rank 1 gets this, rank 2 gets this, and rank 3 gets that.

180
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:45,740
So again, this is not directly used,

181
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:48,610
but scatter is an important stepping stone

182
00:09:48,610 --> 00:09:50,190
to understand reduce-scatter.

183
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:54,690
So, as the name implies, scatter just takes a big tensor

184
00:09:54,690 --> 00:09:59,610
at one place and spreads it out onto multiple places.

185
00:09:59,610 --> 00:10:01,810
And you can see how this might be helpful,

186
00:10:01,810 --> 00:10:05,930
because you want all the GPUs you're

187
00:10:05,930 --> 00:10:10,790
scattering to do some local computation on the different parts.

188
00:10:13,690 --> 00:10:17,170
So the inverse of scatter is gather.

189
00:10:17,170 --> 00:10:20,010
So this should be very predictable.

190
00:10:20,010 --> 00:10:24,170
The input is you have a bunch of pieces, each of which

191
00:10:24,170 --> 00:10:26,690
reside on a particular rank.

192
00:10:26,690 --> 00:10:29,770
And then when you do a gather, that's

193
00:10:29,770 --> 00:10:32,810
with respect to a particular rank, rank 0,

194
00:10:32,810 --> 00:10:35,430
it's going to just concatenate all the pieces together.

195
00:10:38,170 --> 00:10:40,510
Again, gather isn't directly used,

196
00:10:40,510 --> 00:10:45,530
but it's going to be a stepping stone to understand all-gather.

197
00:10:45,530 --> 00:10:47,470
So next is reduce.

198
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:50,970
So those of you who do functional programming are

199
00:10:50,970 --> 00:10:52,770
probably familiar with what reduce is.

200
00:10:52,770 --> 00:10:54,930
It's exactly the same.

201
00:10:54,930 --> 00:10:58,450
The idea is that you start in the same starting point

202
00:10:58,450 --> 00:11:02,330
as a gather.

203
00:11:02,330 --> 00:11:07,490
And where the tensor is split up across multiple-- well,

204
00:11:07,490 --> 00:11:10,930
you have some piece of data on each of the different ranks.

205
00:11:10,930 --> 00:11:13,250
And then you're going to apply your reduction

206
00:11:13,250 --> 00:11:20,010
operation to all of these and get that in, put that on rank 0.

207
00:11:20,010 --> 00:11:23,250
So in this case, if you do a reduction with sum,

208
00:11:23,250 --> 00:11:26,810
then you add these all up, you get six.

209
00:11:26,810 --> 00:11:29,530
So you can think about gather as a reduction

210
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:34,390
where the operation is concatenation, if you will.

211
00:11:37,170 --> 00:11:39,850
And of course, reduce is important to understanding

212
00:11:39,850 --> 00:11:41,650
all reduce.

213
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:43,810
So let me pause there.

214
00:11:43,810 --> 00:11:46,290
Those were just the warm ups. Just in case people

215
00:11:46,290 --> 00:11:50,850
have any questions about what a collective operation is,

216
00:11:50,850 --> 00:11:54,510
broadcast, scatter, gather, reduce.

217
00:11:59,850 --> 00:12:01,450
Yeah?

218
00:12:01,450 --> 00:12:06,250
[INAUDIBLE] NumPy, where is it?

219
00:12:06,250 --> 00:12:08,290
So the question is, is this related

220
00:12:08,290 --> 00:12:12,410
to broadcasting and NumPy?

221
00:12:12,410 --> 00:12:17,090
I mean, I think it's conceptually the same idea,

222
00:12:17,090 --> 00:12:19,650
where you have one thing that goes to many things.

223
00:12:19,650 --> 00:12:21,190
Like in NumPy, if you have a scalar,

224
00:12:21,190 --> 00:12:22,770
get broadcast to a tensor.

225
00:12:22,770 --> 00:12:27,670
But the instantiation, this is for collective communication.

226
00:12:27,670 --> 00:12:29,030
So it's a bit different.

227
00:12:32,650 --> 00:12:35,950
So let's move on to something more interesting.

228
00:12:35,950 --> 00:12:41,090
So all gather is what you do to-- basically,

229
00:12:41,090 --> 00:12:45,160
you perform gather to all ranks, not just rank 0.

230
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:47,040
So remember what gather does.

231
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:51,280
It basically takes all the different pieces

232
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:55,480
and it just puts it on one rank, rank 0.

233
00:12:55,480 --> 00:13:00,320
And now all gather just does it for every single rank.

234
00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:01,900
That's what the all is for.

235
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:06,040
All means do it for all the output to all the ranks,

236
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:08,540
and gather is what you're doing to all the ranks.

237
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:16,160
So this is going to come up a bunch.

238
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:18,440
It's not important that you understand this statement

239
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:23,160
precisely, but later we'll see that each rank holds

240
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:25,040
part of the parameters.

241
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:27,720
And then what you need to do is all

242
00:13:27,720 --> 00:13:30,160
gather the parameters to get the full parameters

243
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:32,160
for the full forward pass.

244
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:34,380
So in general, as we're doing training,

245
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:38,160
we're going to see a lot of this gather to do something

246
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:43,040
and then scatter and then gather and scatter again.

247
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:49,720
So reduce scatter is performing reduce on each dimension

248
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:52,800
and then scattering the results.

249
00:13:52,800 --> 00:14:02,320
So let's say you have four devices and each of them

250
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:05,400
has some vector.

251
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:12,260
And so when we did a reduce before, we just had 0123,

252
00:14:12,260 --> 00:14:14,520
and that got reduced to 6.

253
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:17,720
But now reduce scatter says that for each component

254
00:14:17,720 --> 00:14:20,720
of this tensor, I'm going to do a reduction,

255
00:14:20,720 --> 00:14:24,620
and then I'm going to put it on a different rank.

256
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:33,625
So the first dimension, I'm going to add these up, I get 6.

257
00:14:33,625 --> 00:14:36,000
Now, for the second dimension, I'm going to add these up.

258
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:37,000
I get 10.

259
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,012
For third dimension, I'm going to process these.

260
00:14:39,012 --> 00:14:41,220
For the fourth dimension, I'm going to process those.

261
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:52,440
So where this is going to show up, just to foreshadow things,

262
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:57,440
after the backward pass, when you sum what you're going to do

263
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:02,640
is each GPU will be dealing with different data.

264
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:07,760
And what you're doing is you need to sum all of the gradients

265
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:10,160
from the different shards.

266
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:14,820
And then you're going to redistribute this storage.

267
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:21,080
And then finally, all reduce.

268
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:25,280
If you understand all reduce, gather and all gather,

269
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:28,740
it's basically you do one and then you do the other.

270
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:35,280
So what this does is reduce scatter, the same input as we

271
00:15:35,280 --> 00:15:37,640
had before.

272
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:41,520
And remember, in reduce scatter, we

273
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:46,120
had 6, 10, 14, and 18 sit on different ranks.

274
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:49,280
And the all gather part of all reduce just

275
00:15:49,280 --> 00:15:53,700
puts them all on the same node.

276
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:59,620
So all reduce is in some sense the easiest to understand.

277
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:01,460
You have a bunch of tensors.

278
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:03,680
You reduce, in this case, sum.

279
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:05,940
And then you replicate them on all the nodes.

280
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:12,520
So we're going to see this one actually first

281
00:16:12,520 --> 00:16:16,240
当我们进行数据并行并对梯度求和时

282
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:20,560
然后复制完整的参数。

283
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:22,660
所以这就是我们真正要开始的地方。

284
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:25,040
所以也许只需把注意力集中在 all reduce 上。

285
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:30,800
稍后，我们将看到如何实现更复杂的东西，比如 0

286
00:16:30,800 --> 00:16:32,280
或 FSDP。

287
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:36,440
我们需要把 all reduce 拆分为 reduce scatter 和 all

288
00:16:36,440 --> 00:16:39,190
gather，因为这样你才能介入

289
00:16:39,190 --> 00:16:41,410
并且可以更好地管理这些事情。

290
00:16:41,410 --> 00:16:45,530
但对于基本版本，all reduce 就够了。

291
00:16:48,390 --> 00:16:51,710
最后，all to all。

292
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:54,370
这个在某种程度上是最通用的。

293
00:16:54,370 --> 00:17:00,190
你基本上指定每个 rank 如何发送特定消息

294
00:17:00,190 --> 00:17:03,230
到另一个 rank。

295
00:17:03,230 --> 00:17:06,710
所以这里有一个简单的例子，其中你

296
00:17:06,710 --> 00:17:09,310
拥有和之前相同的输入。

297
00:17:09,310 --> 00:17:11,550
它表达的意思是，我

298
00:17:11,550 --> 00:17:17,790
想把 0 发送到这个元素到 rank 0，

299
00:17:17,790 --> 00:17:19,550
也就是保留在我自己这里。

300
00:17:19,550 --> 00:17:22,829
我要把一个发送到 rank 1，两个

301
00:17:22,829 --> 00:17:25,829
发送到 rank 2，三个发送到 rank 3。

302
00:17:25,829 --> 00:17:31,150
然后如果我是 rank 1，我想把四个发送到 rank 0，五个

303
00:17:31,150 --> 00:17:36,110
发送到 rank 1，六个发送到 rank 2，七个发送到 rank 3。

304
00:17:36,110 --> 00:17:39,230
所以基本上，这里的位置

305
00:17:39,230 --> 00:17:42,230
将表示哪个 rank

306
00:17:42,230 --> 00:17:44,670
会成为最终目的地。

307
00:17:44,670 --> 00:17:49,230
所以如果你看输出，会发生的是

308
00:17:49,230 --> 00:17:54,950
第一个 rank 将接收所有

309
00:17:54,950 --> 00:18:00,390
在列 0 中发送内容的人，因为所有这些 rank 都

310
00:18:00,390 --> 00:18:03,510
把这些东西发送到 rank 0。

311
00:18:03,510 --> 00:18:08,670
同样，对于 rank 1，所有的 rank

312
00:18:08,670 --> 00:18:12,370
都发送这一列到 rank 1，依此类推。

313
00:18:16,870 --> 00:18:22,710
所以这在训练 MOE 时会很有用。

314
00:18:22,710 --> 00:18:26,350
这里的直觉是，每个 rank

315
00:18:26,350 --> 00:18:29,990
既有数据的一个分片，也有一组专家子集。

316
00:18:29,990 --> 00:18:34,070
基本上，MOE 的关键思想

317
00:18:34,070 --> 00:18:36,170
是它是动态路由。

318
00:18:36,170 --> 00:18:38,390
你必须查看你的数据，以确定

319
00:18:38,390 --> 00:18:44,630
你需要把这些激活值路由到哪些专家。

320
00:18:44,630 --> 00:18:49,950
所以它最终是一种 all to all 通信。

321
00:18:49,950 --> 00:18:56,190
So if everything were balanced, meaning that every rank sent

322
00:18:56,190 --> 00:19:00,805
the same number of bytes to every other rank,

323
00:19:00,805 --> 00:19:02,430
then all-to-all, you can think about it

324
00:19:02,430 --> 00:19:03,942
as essentially a transpose.

325
00:19:03,942 --> 00:19:06,150
If you think about this as a matrix, all you're doing

326
00:19:06,150 --> 00:19:08,950
is transposing that matrix.

327
00:19:08,950 --> 00:19:13,710
But in general, all-to-all also handles unbalanced splits.

328
00:19:13,710 --> 00:19:16,030
And I'm not showing this here, but you

329
00:19:16,030 --> 00:19:19,390
can configure it to send any number of bytes

330
00:19:19,390 --> 00:19:23,230
to any other rank.

331
00:19:23,230 --> 00:19:24,990
But in general, you want the splits

332
00:19:24,990 --> 00:19:27,290
to be as balanced as possible.

333
00:19:27,290 --> 00:19:29,550
So remember the MoE lecture

334
00:19:29,550 --> 00:19:32,980
where we had load balancing to make sure that things were as

335
00:19:32,980 --> 00:19:34,340
balanced as possible.

336
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:38,820
So morally, the ideal goal is to have all-to-all

337
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:40,280
look kind of like this.

338
00:19:44,320 --> 00:19:48,700
So just to summarize, maybe a few helpful tips to remember

339
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:52,700
the terminology, because I just went through quite

340
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:56,660
a few different operations.

341
00:19:56,660 --> 00:20:00,040
So reduce is, well, it's reduced.

342
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:04,400
It performs some associative commutative operation.

343
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:07,580
Could be sum, could be max, could be min.

344
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:09,660
Scatter is the inverse of gather.

345
00:20:09,660 --> 00:20:12,780
Scatter distributes, gather centralizes.

346
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:17,120
And all just means that the destination is all devices.

347
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:23,440
So that explains all-reduce and all-gather.

348
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:26,300
Let me pause there to take any questions

349
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:28,320
about collective communications.

350
00:20:31,780 --> 00:20:32,420
Yeah?

351
00:20:32,420 --> 00:20:37,980
For operations such as gathering where we basically

352
00:20:37,980 --> 00:20:42,300
to rank 0, does that rank 0?

353
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:47,680
Is it a particular GPU every time or can that rank 0 change?

354
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:48,180
Yeah.

355
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:52,540
So the question is when you do a gather or a reduce,

356
00:20:52,540 --> 00:21:00,580
the target where you write the output, right now I said rank 0.

357
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:02,660
You'll see later in the code, you basically

358
00:21:02,660 --> 00:21:10,180
specify the GPU ID or the rank and it goes there.

359
00:21:10,180 --> 00:21:13,220
So it doesn't have to be determined way in advance,

360
00:21:13,220 --> 00:21:15,940
but it has to be determined basically

361
00:21:15,940 --> 00:21:18,240
when you execute the call.

362
00:21:23,340 --> 00:21:24,760
Cool.

363
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:27,060
Anything else?

364
00:21:27,060 --> 00:21:27,980
Yeah.

365
00:21:27,980 --> 00:21:30,580
These are just conceptual building blocks, right?

366
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:34,940
They're not actually [INAUDIBLE].

367
00:21:34,940 --> 00:21:40,060
Are these the actual [INAUDIBLE]?

368
00:21:40,060 --> 00:21:42,820
Yeah, so the question is, are these just conceptual building

369
00:21:42,820 --> 00:21:44,040
blocks or are they code?

370
00:21:44,040 --> 00:21:46,740
So I'm showing these right now as just conceptual building

371
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:48,740
blocks, but we'll very quickly see how

372
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:50,320
these are implemented in code.

373
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:56,100
So before getting into the code, I

374
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:58,940
want to talk a bit about the hardware,

375
00:21:58,940 --> 00:22:01,140
in particular how GPUs are connected,

376
00:22:01,140 --> 00:22:04,940
because we already what's inside a GPU.

377
00:22:04,940 --> 00:22:10,100
So let's talk about networking in general.

378
00:22:10,100 --> 00:22:12,360
So this is a very classic picture.

379
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:17,340
You can tell from this very old looking image

380
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:22,580
that this is how computers, I mean, generally work.

381
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:26,840
So you have this server and then you have a bunch of CPUs.

382
00:22:26,840 --> 00:22:31,660
There's a PCIe bus, which you connect things like your--

383
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:35,700
or used to connect things like your mouse and keyboard.

384
00:22:35,700 --> 00:22:39,180
And then you have a bunch of GPUs sitting off of them

385
00:22:39,180 --> 00:22:40,820
and you have some RAM.

386
00:22:40,820 --> 00:22:43,060
And then this computer is connected

387
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:47,020
to ethernet to another computer, and so on and so forth.

388
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:50,440
So this is a particular setup.

389
00:22:50,440 --> 00:22:52,940
It's a particular topology.

390
00:22:52,940 --> 00:23:00,060
And GPUs on the same node, you use PCIe to communicate and GPUs

391
00:23:00,060 --> 00:23:04,340
on different nodes, you have to go all the way through ethernet.

392
00:23:04,340 --> 00:23:07,620
So this is like if you bought your gaming GPU

393
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:10,240
and you had hooked it up with your friend and was like,

394
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:12,500
I'm going to train some big model.

395
00:23:12,500 --> 00:23:14,160
That's what you would have to do.

396
00:23:14,160 --> 00:23:17,540
But if you're really serious about training,

397
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:21,600
then things look more like this.

398
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:25,180
This is the picture I showed in the very beginning

399
00:23:25,180 --> 00:23:27,210
where you have the GPU and there's

400
00:23:27,210 --> 00:23:31,330
something called NVLink and NVSwitch and InfiniBand.

401
00:23:31,330 --> 00:23:35,930
So the typical setup is this.

402
00:23:35,930 --> 00:23:38,030
And these numbers, eight is typical,

403
00:23:38,030 --> 00:23:40,930
but this 256 is kind of made up.

404
00:23:40,930 --> 00:23:44,850
So typically, you have eight GPUs per node.

405
00:23:44,850 --> 00:23:52,330
And these are connected via NVIDIA's NVLink to a switch.

406
00:23:52,330 --> 00:23:56,850
And just for calibration, if you use NVLink 5,

407
00:23:56,850 --> 00:24:00,330
then you're getting 1.8 terabytes

408
00:24:00,330 --> 00:24:03,250
per second of total bandwidth.

409
00:24:03,250 --> 00:24:08,790
And remember, HBM for B200 was 8 terabytes per second.

410
00:24:08,790 --> 00:24:13,970
So it's about 4x slower.

411
00:24:13,970 --> 00:24:15,970
So I mean, this is still pretty fast

412
00:24:15,970 --> 00:24:19,650
if you think about going between devices, but obviously,

413
00:24:19,650 --> 00:24:22,590
not as fast as high bandwidth memory,

414
00:24:22,590 --> 00:24:28,450
which is much slower than shared memory or L1 cache.

415
00:24:28,450 --> 00:24:33,290
So basically, NVLink connects to the switch,

416
00:24:33,290 --> 00:24:36,730
which means that from a programming perspective,

417
00:24:36,730 --> 00:24:40,830
you can think about GPUs as connected to any other GPU.

418
00:24:40,830 --> 00:24:44,250
You go GPU to any other GPU, and the hardware

419
00:24:44,250 --> 00:24:47,550
takes care of transmitting that to the switch,

420
00:24:47,550 --> 00:24:51,530
and the switch routes it.

421
00:24:51,530 --> 00:24:56,050
So typically, what you will also have is that at some point,

422
00:24:56,050 --> 00:25:02,330
you can't have NVSwitch and NVLink.

423
00:25:02,330 --> 00:25:06,010
Because as your number of GPUs grows,

424
00:25:06,010 --> 00:25:11,650
then you're going to have to put these nodes into pods, which

425
00:25:11,650 --> 00:25:14,490
are connected by InfiniBand.

426
00:25:14,490 --> 00:25:18,530
And the way InfiniBand works is that now there's

427
00:25:18,530 --> 00:25:24,010
a bit more the-- so now the GPU doesn't connect directly

428
00:25:24,010 --> 00:25:25,390
to another GPU.

429
00:25:25,390 --> 00:25:30,890
It has to go through PCIe and goes through this kind

430
00:25:30,890 --> 00:25:32,750
of special InfiniBand cable.

431
00:25:32,750 --> 00:25:39,370
And you see that the speeds are much, much lower.

432
00:25:39,370 --> 00:25:45,770
And then finally, if you run out of InfiniBand you have these huge pods, then

433
00:25:45,770 --> 00:25:49,690
you need to connect them via ethernet.

434
00:25:49,690 --> 00:25:55,050
And in ethernet, you have to go through PCIe

435
00:25:55,050 --> 00:25:58,690
and that actually goes through the CPU, which as we'll see,

436
00:25:58,690 --> 00:26:02,810
is even slower.

437
00:26:02,810 --> 00:26:06,590
So it's kind of analogous to the kind of memory situation.

438
00:26:06,590 --> 00:26:11,070
The more nodes you have, then the slower it's going to be.

439
00:26:11,070 --> 00:26:18,850
You can't have an NVSwitch handling 100,000 GPUs.

440
00:26:18,850 --> 00:26:23,450
So one note is that I alluded to this bypassing

441
00:26:23,450 --> 00:26:29,050
the CPU, which is going to be an important thing from a hardware

442
00:26:29,050 --> 00:26:31,730
perspective.

443
00:26:31,730 --> 00:26:35,330
So if you have traditional ethernet, what happens

444
00:26:35,330 --> 00:26:40,950
is that the GPU has to talk to the CPU to get its data copied.

445
00:26:40,950 --> 00:26:45,370
So it basically has to copy the data to this.

446
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:47,910
The CPU has a kernel socket buffer.

447
00:26:47,910 --> 00:26:50,750
Here kernel means not the GPU kernel,

448
00:26:50,750 --> 00:26:54,250
but the CPU, traditional notion of a kernel.

449
00:26:54,250 --> 00:26:57,430
And then it has to build some network packets,

450
00:26:57,430 --> 00:27:00,450
copy to the network interface, and then ship it over.

451
00:27:00,450 --> 00:27:05,010
So this generally introduces a lot of latency.

452
00:27:05,010 --> 00:27:08,010
And so there's this technology called Remote Direct Memory

453
00:27:08,010 --> 00:27:13,570
Access, RDMA, which allows a GPU to directly write or read

454
00:27:13,570 --> 00:27:19,450
from another GPU's memory without using the CPU at all.

455
00:27:19,450 --> 00:27:23,720
So obviously, if you're in NVLink land and NVSwitch land,

456
00:27:23,720 --> 00:27:26,000
then you have RDMA.

457
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,840
InfiniBand also supports RDMA.

458
00:27:28,840 --> 00:27:30,760
So if you're connected via InfiniBand,

459
00:27:30,760 --> 00:27:34,440
then you can directly have GPUs connect to each other

460
00:27:34,440 --> 00:27:37,880
without involving the CPU.

461
00:27:37,880 --> 00:27:42,360
But standard ethernet does not.

462
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:45,960
There's two notable advancements I will mention.

463
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:51,280
So NVIDIA has been really pushing

464
00:27:51,280 --> 00:27:54,940
the limits on what you can do with larger and larger pods.

465
00:27:54,940 --> 00:27:59,880
So they have four of basically the B200's and the B300's.

466
00:27:59,880 --> 00:28:02,360
They have something called NVL72,

467
00:28:02,360 --> 00:28:06,960
which means that they have these trays of eight GPUs

468
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:08,340
but have nine of them.

469
00:28:08,340 --> 00:28:10,020
And so basically, at the end of the day,

470
00:28:10,020 --> 00:28:13,720
you have 72 GPUs that are all kind of NV switched

471
00:28:13,720 --> 00:28:15,220
into one NVLink domain.

472
00:28:15,220 --> 00:28:22,080
And if you remember, the NVLink speeds are very fast.

473
00:28:22,080 --> 00:28:26,320
So normally, if you're mortal, you

474
00:28:26,320 --> 00:28:29,320
think, well OK, I have eight GPUs that

475
00:28:29,320 --> 00:28:31,060
are interlinked really fast.

476
00:28:31,060 --> 00:28:35,182
And then outside of that, then things get slowed down a lot.

477
00:28:35,182 --> 00:28:36,640
But if you have a lot of money, you

478
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:39,040
can buy this really fancy hardware

479
00:28:39,040 --> 00:28:46,182
and you can get really fast interconnects up to 72 GPUs.

480
00:28:46,182 --> 00:28:47,640
And the other thing I'll mention is

481
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:51,680
我说过标准以太网不支持 RDMA，

482
00:28:51,680 --> 00:28:55,100
但以太网这方面也有了进展。

483
00:28:55,100 --> 00:28:59,240
所以有一种东西叫 RoCE，即 RDMA

484
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:02,840
over converged ethernet，其中以太网实际上

485
00:29:02,840 --> 00:29:05,840
绕过了 CPU。

486
00:29:05,840 --> 00:29:10,660
这算是他们对 InfiniBand 的回应。

487
00:29:10,660 --> 00:29:13,600
InfiniBand 通常非常昂贵，

488
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:17,040
很多 NVIDIA 产品也是如此。

489
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:21,880
但你可以获得相当不错的性能

490
00:29:21,880 --> 00:29:29,680
通过使用 RDMA over converged ethernet。

491
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:35,020
Meta 有一些论文表明他们正在探索这个方向。

492
00:29:35,020 --> 00:29:38,480
所以 [INAUDIBLE] 可能是也可能不是

493
00:29:38,480 --> 00:29:40,780
通过 converged ethernet 训练的。

494
00:29:43,380 --> 00:29:47,540
这只是对硬件外观的简要概述。

495
00:29:47,540 --> 00:29:48,620
你有 GPU。

496
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:53,800
它们通过 NVLink 连接到 NVSwitch，在一个域内，

497
00:29:53,800 --> 00:29:55,440
可能是 8 个，也可能是 72 个。

498
00:29:55,440 --> 00:29:58,920
然后从那里开始用 InfiniBand。

499
00:29:58,920 --> 00:30:03,120
现在我们来谈谈如何对这个进行编程。

500
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:05,840
所以在最低层，有一种东西叫

501
00:30:05,840 --> 00:30:09,000
NVIDIA Collective Communications Library，

502
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:12,560
NCCL 或叫 "nickel"，它把集体

503
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:17,030
操作，比如 all reduce、reduce broadcast，

504
00:30:17,030 --> 00:30:22,230
转换成 GPU 之间发送的实际底层数据包。

505
00:30:22,230 --> 00:30:27,650
NCCL 的作用是，当你使用 NCCL 时，

506
00:30:27,650 --> 00:30:31,070
基本上就像在说，我想要 all reduce。

507
00:30:31,070 --> 00:30:33,910
然后 NCCL 会去弄清楚硬件

508
00:30:33,910 --> 00:30:36,670
的拓扑结构，找出

509
00:30:36,670 --> 00:30:39,470
不同 GPU 之间的路径，然后

510
00:30:39,470 --> 00:30:42,430
它实际上会启动 GPU kernel 来发送和接收

511
00:30:42,430 --> 00:30:43,483
数据。

512
00:30:43,483 --> 00:30:45,150
因为归根结底，记住，

513
00:30:45,150 --> 00:30:47,910
在 GPU 上运行的一切都是 kernel。

514
00:30:47,910 --> 00:30:50,870
所以也有通信 kernel，

515
00:30:50,870 --> 00:30:56,070
它们实际上与其他 GPU 进行通信。

516
00:30:56,070 --> 00:30:59,610
所以我们不会深入研究 NCCL，

517
00:30:59,610 --> 00:31:01,890
只需要知道它存在。

518
00:31:04,710 --> 00:31:07,330
然后我们实际上会转到 PyTorch。

519
00:31:07,330 --> 00:31:13,230
所以也许在那之前，关于硬件有什么问题吗？

520
00:31:13,230 --> 00:31:14,190
是的。

521
00:31:14,190 --> 00:31:18,710
Can you describe—there's a rack and a tray.

522
00:31:21,830 --> 00:31:25,230
Can I describe physically a rack and a tray, like what a rack is

523
00:31:25,230 --> 00:31:28,110
and what a tray is?

524
00:31:28,110 --> 00:31:32,910
So for the NVL72, so I'm not a hardware expert,

525
00:31:32,910 --> 00:31:37,990
but a rack, I mean, is literally, I mean,

526
00:31:37,990 --> 00:31:41,450
you've seen data centers, literally a rack.

527
00:31:41,450 --> 00:31:45,230
And each tray is something that has—

528
00:31:45,230 --> 00:31:50,430
so the G stands for a [? great. ?] So there's two CPUs.

529
00:31:50,430 --> 00:31:53,090
And each CPU is connected to four GPUs.

530
00:31:53,090 --> 00:31:57,490
So each tray has eight GPUs on it.

531
00:31:57,490 --> 00:32:00,110
And they're stacked and everything

532
00:32:00,110 --> 00:32:03,090
is connected to this NVSwitch.

533
00:32:07,070 --> 00:32:07,910
Yeah.

534
00:32:15,337 --> 00:32:17,670
Yeah, so the question is, what is the difference between

535
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:22,410
Yeah.

536
00:32:22,410 --> 00:32:27,610
So RDMA, you can think about it as more of a desideratum.

537
00:32:27,610 --> 00:32:30,630
RDMA means that one GPU can read and write

538
00:32:30,630 --> 00:32:32,710
from another GPU's memory.

539
00:32:32,710 --> 00:32:35,790
And there's multiple ways to do RDMA.

540
00:32:35,790 --> 00:32:39,350
One is to use NVLink and NVSwitch

541
00:32:39,350 --> 00:32:42,050
and another way is to use InfiniBand.

542
00:32:44,910 --> 00:32:48,710
So InfiniBand and NVSwitch and NVLink

543
00:32:48,710 --> 00:32:55,550
are more the hardware, what pieces, what cables and switches

544
00:32:55,550 --> 00:32:56,630
are there.

545
00:32:56,630 --> 00:33:02,590
And RDMA is more operation, what happens

546
00:33:02,590 --> 00:33:04,030
when you're communicating.

547
00:33:15,210 --> 00:33:15,710
Yeah.

548
00:33:15,710 --> 00:33:18,830
For example, this [? advancement ?] RDMA

549
00:33:18,830 --> 00:33:21,850
over converged ethernet is another way to do RDMA.

550
00:33:32,610 --> 00:33:33,350
Yeah?

551
00:33:33,350 --> 00:33:39,470
Is NCCL optimized for multi-node clusters?

552
00:33:39,470 --> 00:33:43,350
Because obviously, it seems intuitive [INAUDIBLE].

553
00:33:43,350 --> 00:33:50,950
But for RDMA based processes, would NCCL be optimal for that?

554
00:33:50,950 --> 00:33:53,510
So the question is, is NCCL optimized

555
00:33:53,510 --> 00:33:56,770
for multi-node clusters?

556
00:33:59,710 --> 00:34:05,750
So I don't know the details of how they have or have not

557
00:34:05,750 --> 00:34:06,770
optimized it.

558
00:34:06,770 --> 00:34:12,380
All I can say is that NVIDIA has been basically optimizing

559
00:34:12,380 --> 00:34:16,179
their entire stack for inference and training

560
00:34:16,179 --> 00:34:19,500
of these large models, because their main customers are

561
00:34:19,500 --> 00:34:23,360
the major providers of language models.

562
00:34:23,360 --> 00:34:27,300
So I would be surprised if they haven't thought of optimizing

563
00:34:27,300 --> 00:34:29,080
for those type of workloads.

564
00:34:31,940 --> 00:34:32,820
Yeah?

565
00:34:44,260 --> 00:34:47,760
So the question is, what happens if you have nine GPUs?

566
00:34:47,760 --> 00:34:51,760
How do you distribute the workload across those?

567
00:34:55,980 --> 00:34:57,740
I guess it kind of depends on how

568
00:34:57,740 --> 00:35:00,740
the nine lands in your setup.

569
00:35:00,740 --> 00:35:03,180
For example, a lot of times you'll have--

570
00:35:03,180 --> 00:35:06,980
let's say you have eight GPUs per node.

571
00:35:06,980 --> 00:35:09,340
So then the ninth one would be on a different node.

572
00:35:09,340 --> 00:35:14,480
And if you don't have NVLink connecting them,

573
00:35:14,480 --> 00:35:16,980
then that's going to be really bad, because that's

574
00:35:16,980 --> 00:35:18,900
going to be one node which is not providing

575
00:35:18,900 --> 00:35:23,860
that much compute and also very expensive to communicate with.

576
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:27,300
But if you, let's say, had everything

577
00:35:27,300 --> 00:35:29,860
were connected by NVSwitch, then it

578
00:35:29,860 --> 00:35:33,980
would be much more reasonable.

579
00:35:33,980 --> 00:35:36,340
One more question and I'm going to move on.

580
00:35:39,060 --> 00:35:43,020
So how is this different from TPUs?

581
00:35:43,020 --> 00:35:47,420
So [INAUDIBLE] described TPUs a bit more.

582
00:35:47,420 --> 00:35:53,220
So I mean, let's see.

583
00:35:53,220 --> 00:35:56,940
What can I say quickly about this?

584
00:35:56,940 --> 00:36:03,180
So TPUs are generally much simpler objects.

585
00:36:03,180 --> 00:36:07,460
I'm not too familiar with the details of what

586
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:09,440
each of these components corresponds to,

587
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:11,320
but maybe we can talk about it offline.

588
00:36:13,900 --> 00:36:16,460
So let's actually get down to some code

589
00:36:16,460 --> 00:36:20,320
to take advantage of this hardware.

590
00:36:20,320 --> 00:36:23,500
So PyTorch conveniently has a torch

591
00:36:23,500 --> 00:36:25,740
distributed library that provides

592
00:36:25,740 --> 00:36:29,540
a clean interface into these collective operations.

593
00:36:29,540 --> 00:36:34,140
So you don't have to explicitly think about NCCL.

594
00:36:34,140 --> 00:36:38,100
And in fact, this library also supports different backends

595
00:36:38,100 --> 00:36:39,660
for different hardware.

596
00:36:39,660 --> 00:36:43,180
So if you are on GPUs, then you would use the NCCL backend.

597
00:36:43,180 --> 00:36:45,260
And if you were on CPUs, then there's

598
00:36:45,260 --> 00:36:48,720
something called gloo which still allows you to--

599
00:36:48,720 --> 00:36:51,300
Like I said, parallel processing has been around

600
00:36:51,300 --> 00:36:54,900
for a long time before GPUs.

601
00:36:54,900 --> 00:37:01,380
And you can do these collective operations on CPUs as well.

602
00:37:01,380 --> 00:37:04,140
This library also supports higher level models

603
00:37:04,140 --> 00:37:08,050
and algorithms such as SFTP, but we're not

604
00:37:08,050 --> 00:37:10,770
going to use those in the course because we're building things

605
00:37:10,770 --> 00:37:12,770
from scratch.

606
00:37:12,770 --> 00:37:13,310
All right.

607
00:37:13,310 --> 00:37:16,770
So let's walk through some basic examples

608
00:37:16,770 --> 00:37:19,570
of collective operations.

609
00:37:19,570 --> 00:37:22,210
So there's this function called spawn,

610
00:37:22,210 --> 00:37:25,130
which takes another function I'm going to call,

611
00:37:25,130 --> 00:37:30,210
and it says I'm going to run this replicated four times where

612
00:37:30,210 --> 00:37:32,090
four is the world size.

613
00:37:32,090 --> 00:37:34,770
So let's see what this does.

614
00:37:34,770 --> 00:37:38,970
This is actually a wrapper I wrote just

615
00:37:38,970 --> 00:37:43,130
to hack around the fact that I can't do multiprocessing

616
00:37:43,130 --> 00:37:44,770
in this lecture.

617
00:37:44,770 --> 00:37:47,370
So normally what you would do is you

618
00:37:47,370 --> 00:37:56,010
call Torch's multiprocessing.spawn and you call the function.

619
00:37:56,010 --> 00:38:00,710
But I'm going to do this branch which disables distributed.

620
00:38:00,710 --> 00:38:02,650
So let's just go through that.

621
00:38:02,650 --> 00:38:06,930
So now I'm in this function that it's

622
00:38:06,930 --> 00:38:11,850
supposed to be running asynchronously for each process.

623
00:38:11,850 --> 00:38:15,010
So remember, world size is the number of processes

624
00:38:15,010 --> 00:38:20,210
and the rank is either 0 or 1 or 2 all the way up to world

625
00:38:20,210 --> 00:38:21,690
size minus 1.

626
00:38:21,690 --> 00:38:23,450
And so there are world size number

627
00:38:23,450 --> 00:38:25,410
of these functions that are each running

628
00:38:25,410 --> 00:38:28,170
on a process at the same time.

629
00:38:28,170 --> 00:38:31,110
So I'm on rank 0 right now.

630
00:38:31,110 --> 00:38:33,090
So what do I do?

631
00:38:33,090 --> 00:38:35,010
There's a setup.

632
00:38:35,010 --> 00:38:41,450
You basically configure the master address and port.

633
00:38:41,450 --> 00:38:45,115
Notice that this is not actually how the GPUs are

634
00:38:45,115 --> 00:38:45,990
going to communicate.

635
00:38:45,990 --> 00:38:49,750
This is more for general metadata and coordination.

636
00:38:49,750 --> 00:38:51,630
实际数据会经过 NCCL。

637
00:38:51,630 --> 00:38:54,970
否则会非常非常慢。

638
00:38:54,970 --> 00:38:57,530
如果你有 CUDA 可用，那么你

639
00:38:57,530 --> 00:38:59,610
可以使用 NCCL 后端。

640
00:38:59,610 --> 00:39:04,250
我在自己的笔记本上，所以我会使用 gloo 后端。

641
00:39:04,250 --> 00:39:08,690
好，现在我来这里。

642
00:39:08,690 --> 00:39:13,050
假设我有四个这样的进程在运行。

643
00:39:13,050 --> 00:39:19,050
所以有一个 barrier 函数，它的用处是——

644
00:39:19,050 --> 00:39:20,510
它是一个同步屏障。

645
00:39:20,510 --> 00:39:24,410
所以基本上，如果我看到这个，那么它

646
00:39:24,410 --> 00:39:27,450
会等待所有进程到达这个点。

647
00:39:30,410 --> 00:39:33,570
所以基本上，你可以认为所有进程

648
00:39:33,570 --> 00:39:35,687
都在异步运行。

649
00:39:35,687 --> 00:39:36,770
所以我并不真正控制。

650
00:39:36,770 --> 00:39:39,070
一个进程可能完全在另一个之前完成。

651
00:39:39,070 --> 00:39:42,330
它们可能以任意方式交错完成。

652
00:39:42,330 --> 00:39:45,130
所以如果我想确保

653
00:39:45,130 --> 00:39:48,890
有些代码在其他代码之前执行，

654
00:39:48,890 --> 00:39:52,490
我就会加入这些同步屏障。

655
00:39:52,490 --> 00:39:54,850
所以现在加入更多屏障的缺点是

656
00:39:54,850 --> 00:39:58,490
嗯，你最终可能会不必要地

657
00:39:58,490 --> 00:40:01,790
等待。

658
00:40:01,790 --> 00:40:04,610
那么让我们试一下 all reduce。

659
00:40:04,610 --> 00:40:11,090
所以我要创建这个张量，0、1、2、3。

660
00:40:11,090 --> 00:40:13,795
然后我有我的 rank。

661
00:40:13,795 --> 00:40:15,170
为了让它更有意思，

662
00:40:15,170 --> 00:40:20,530
每个 rank 会有一个不同的张量。

663
00:40:20,530 --> 00:40:23,710
我要打印出我在 all reduce 之前有什么。

664
00:40:23,710 --> 00:40:27,410
所以现在我要跳过这里，看看

665
00:40:27,410 --> 00:40:30,490
会打印出什么。

666
00:40:30,490 --> 00:40:36,050
所以 rank 0 在 all reduce 之前有 0、1、2、3。

667
00:40:36,050 --> 00:40:38,310
Rank 1 有 1、2、3、4，依此类推。

668
00:40:38,310 --> 00:40:40,770
这和我之前展示的例子一样。

669
00:40:40,770 --> 00:40:44,130
注意，print 语句以任意顺序出现，

670
00:40:44,130 --> 00:40:47,250
硬件想怎样就怎样，因为它是异步运行的。

671
00:40:47,250 --> 00:40:48,630
但所有数据都在那里。

672
00:40:51,170 --> 00:40:57,410
所以现在如果我做 all reduce，你传入——

673
00:40:57,410 --> 00:40:58,630
这是一个 PyTorch 函数。

674
00:40:58,630 --> 00:41:01,600
它传入这个张量。

675
00:41:01,600 --> 00:41:04,920
你传入归约操作，也就是求和。

676
00:41:04,920 --> 00:41:08,480
And I'm going to say don't do async.

677
00:41:08,480 --> 00:41:12,520
And what this does is it calls, in this case, it would be gloo,

678
00:41:12,520 --> 00:41:15,560
but it could be NCCL, in which case, it

679
00:41:15,560 --> 00:41:18,020
would spin up the CUDA kernels.

680
00:41:18,020 --> 00:41:19,380
It would do the communication.

681
00:41:19,380 --> 00:41:21,880
It takes care of everything for you.

682
00:41:21,880 --> 00:41:26,000
And then it basically writes in place to the data.

683
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:31,380
So after the all reduce, I have the all reduce operation,

684
00:41:31,380 --> 00:41:34,360
remember, which is the sum of each of these columns

685
00:41:34,360 --> 00:41:36,500
but replicated across all the ranks.

686
00:41:40,920 --> 00:41:44,800
So that's all reduce.

687
00:41:44,800 --> 00:41:48,920
And if you wanted to be fancier and do async,

688
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:51,160
then you could say async equals true.

689
00:41:51,160 --> 00:41:53,420
But then it would screw up all these print statements.

690
00:41:53,420 --> 00:41:56,560
So I'm trying to put more barriers than I would normally

691
00:41:56,560 --> 00:41:59,000
would.

692
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:01,040
Yeah, question?

693
00:42:08,560 --> 00:42:11,600
So the question is the rank the GPU.

694
00:42:11,600 --> 00:42:15,180
For this class, the rank is the GPU.

695
00:42:19,340 --> 00:42:22,840
So let's try another example here.

696
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:26,160
So I'm going to do a reduce scatter.

697
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:30,920
So here, I'm going to create an input, which

698
00:42:30,920 --> 00:42:36,080
is 0 through the world size.

699
00:42:36,080 --> 00:42:44,400
And I'm going to have the output.

700
00:42:44,400 --> 00:42:46,440
I'm going to allocate the output.

701
00:42:46,440 --> 00:42:49,880
And so what does this look like before I do the reduce scatter?

702
00:42:49,880 --> 00:42:59,400
It looks like, let's see, this, where it's the same input

703
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:01,380
and the output happens to be zeros,

704
00:43:01,380 --> 00:43:04,640
but it could be just anything.

705
00:43:04,640 --> 00:43:08,680
And then I do the reduce scatter tensor.

706
00:43:08,680 --> 00:43:10,080
Here instead of writing in place,

707
00:43:10,080 --> 00:43:13,200
I have an output tensor and an input tensor.

708
00:43:13,200 --> 00:43:16,520
I say I want to do the sum and then

709
00:43:16,520 --> 00:43:21,700
afterwards, I get basically the input is not touched,

710
00:43:21,700 --> 00:43:25,640
but the output I get the reduction

711
00:43:25,640 --> 00:43:28,900
of each component written into the respective ranks.

712
00:43:36,880 --> 00:43:41,040
So yeah, question?

713
00:43:51,040 --> 00:43:52,800
So the question is, how does it work

714
00:43:52,800 --> 00:43:55,700
to do the all reduce asynchronously?

715
00:43:55,700 --> 00:44:01,340
So what this means is that this is like a monolithic operation.

716
00:44:01,340 --> 00:44:03,320
You say go do the all reduce.

717
00:44:03,320 --> 00:44:06,020
And it's spinning up CUDA kernels.

718
00:44:06,020 --> 00:44:09,400
It's going to do the communication.

719
00:44:09,400 --> 00:44:13,840
And remember, CUDA is already kind of async with respect

720
00:44:13,840 --> 00:44:16,400
to the processes, and now we have

721
00:44:16,400 --> 00:44:18,360
all the processes being async.

722
00:44:18,360 --> 00:44:24,800
And the point is that this code just would return.

723
00:44:24,800 --> 00:44:29,440
And then you could do other things.

724
00:44:29,440 --> 00:44:32,440
So a typical thing, which I'm not

725
00:44:32,440 --> 00:44:36,440
going to talk about this class, is overlapping computation

726
00:44:36,440 --> 00:44:38,360
and communication.

727
00:44:38,360 --> 00:44:43,060
So for example, you can do this operation,

728
00:44:43,060 --> 00:44:45,560
and then you can go ahead and load some other data

729
00:44:45,560 --> 00:44:50,280
for the next step, which is independent of this operation.

730
00:44:50,280 --> 00:44:56,130
And then when you want to make sure that you actually are done,

731
00:44:56,130 --> 00:44:58,610
then you can call a wait or a barrier.

732
00:45:03,990 --> 00:45:08,250
So let's do the final one, which is all gather.

733
00:45:08,250 --> 00:45:10,870
So by now, I think you get the idea.

734
00:45:10,870 --> 00:45:13,430
Here I'm going to set as the input

735
00:45:13,430 --> 00:45:17,230
the output of the reduce scatter.

736
00:45:17,230 --> 00:45:20,110
I'm going to allocate an output.

737
00:45:20,110 --> 00:45:24,050
So before the all gather, it looks like this.

738
00:45:24,050 --> 00:45:28,630
I have my results from the reduce scatter here.

739
00:45:28,630 --> 00:45:32,250
The output is just allocated.

740
00:45:32,250 --> 00:45:37,790
It happens to have some values in it, but don't worry about it.

741
00:45:37,790 --> 00:45:44,630
And then after I do the all gather into tensor,

742
00:45:44,630 --> 00:45:55,190
and then I will have all the different inputs gathered

743
00:45:55,190 --> 00:45:59,110
onto all the different ranks.

744
00:45:59,110 --> 00:46:02,910
And you can see here that, indeed,

745
00:46:02,910 --> 00:46:05,190
proof via example that all reduce

746
00:46:05,190 --> 00:46:07,970
is equal to reduce scatter plus all gather.

747
00:46:13,270 --> 00:46:20,550
And then just to wrap things up, just as I started by setup, then

748
00:46:20,550 --> 00:46:27,210
I clean up, which it's good practice to clean up.

749
00:46:29,990 --> 00:46:38,070
So that was your first example of a Torch distributed program.

750
00:46:38,070 --> 00:46:44,190
So let's do some benchmarking.

751
00:46:44,190 --> 00:46:49,830
This will be quick, since I want to actually move on to part two.

752
00:46:49,830 --> 00:46:52,910
So how fast does communication happen?

753
00:46:52,910 --> 00:46:55,510
So let's do an all reduce.

754
00:46:55,510 --> 00:47:04,550
所以这里，我要对1亿个元素做all reduce。

755
00:47:04,550 --> 00:47:05,090
哎呀。

756
00:47:09,910 --> 00:47:11,130
抱歉，我把这里弄错了。

757
00:47:14,750 --> 00:47:17,110
所以是all reduce。

758
00:47:17,110 --> 00:47:21,150
所以我要创建这个张量

759
00:47:21,150 --> 00:47:23,790
包含这么多元素。

760
00:47:23,790 --> 00:47:26,350
记住，就像之前做基准测试时一样，

761
00:47:26,350 --> 00:47:28,830
我们首先要预热。

762
00:47:28,830 --> 00:47:32,830
这里我要调用Cuda synchronize，还要调用

763
00:47:32,830 --> 00:47:36,750
barrier，只是为了确保，因为这里有

764
00:47:36,750 --> 00:47:39,190
两种异步形式，Cuda kernel

765
00:47:39,190 --> 00:47:40,408
和不同的进程。

766
00:47:40,408 --> 00:47:41,950
我只是想确保一切

767
00:47:41,950 --> 00:47:49,430
在开始计时之前都不再运行并且已经完成。

768
00:47:49,430 --> 00:47:53,140
然后我要执行all reduce。

769
00:47:53,140 --> 00:47:59,620
然后再用synchronize和barrier等待

770
00:47:59,620 --> 00:48:01,540
并停止计时。

771
00:48:01,540 --> 00:48:08,940
记住，这在每个rank上都会运行。

772
00:48:08,940 --> 00:48:14,200
所以如果我看输出，这里rank 0、2、1、3，

773
00:48:14,200 --> 00:48:16,460
我可能会得到不同的时间，

774
00:48:16,460 --> 00:48:19,100
因为它们都是不同的进程。

775
00:48:19,100 --> 00:48:23,398
每个进程都会报告某个测量值。

776
00:48:23,398 --> 00:48:24,940
如果你想报告一个数字，

777
00:48:24,940 --> 00:48:27,240
你可以取平均值，比如。

778
00:48:30,300 --> 00:48:35,240
现在有一件有用的事情可以做，

779
00:48:35,240 --> 00:48:38,060
这类似于我们之前计算MFU时做的事情，

780
00:48:38,060 --> 00:48:41,900
就是测量有效带宽。

781
00:48:41,900 --> 00:48:47,820
这里的想法是，好吧，这花了1.6毫秒。

782
00:48:47,820 --> 00:48:52,700
这算好还是坏？

783
00:48:52,700 --> 00:48:55,580
所以要计算有效带宽，我们

784
00:48:55,580 --> 00:48:58,220
要做的是计算本质上

785
00:48:58,220 --> 00:49:02,340
在这次计算中发送了多少字节，以及应该

786
00:49:02,340 --> 00:49:05,900
发送多少字节。

787
00:49:05,900 --> 00:49:08,200
然后如果你除以总时间，

788
00:49:08,200 --> 00:49:10,460
就得到有效带宽。

789
00:49:10,460 --> 00:49:14,580
所以我发送的数据大小，

790
00:49:14,580 --> 00:49:18,720
是每个元素的大小乘以元素数量。

791
00:49:18,720 --> 00:49:24,580
所以基本上就是这个数据张量的字节数。

792
00:49:24,580 --> 00:49:26,520
实际发送了多少字节？

793
00:49:26,520 --> 00:49:29,620
所以这需要一些拆解。

794
00:49:29,620 --> 00:49:34,900
So for all reduce, if you think about,

795
00:49:34,900 --> 00:49:39,460
let's just say for simplicity, you do a rank 0 plus rank 1

796
00:49:39,460 --> 00:49:41,820
plus rank 2 plus rank 3.

797
00:49:41,820 --> 00:49:45,420
You need to iterate this world size minus 1 steps,

798
00:49:45,420 --> 00:49:49,820
because there's a world size minus 1 addition operations.

799
00:49:49,820 --> 00:49:51,660
So that's this factor.

800
00:49:51,660 --> 00:49:55,780
There's a 2, because you need to both send and reduce.

801
00:49:55,780 --> 00:49:59,780
And then you multiply by the size of the payload.

802
00:49:59,780 --> 00:50:03,020
So that's the total number of sent bytes.

803
00:50:03,020 --> 00:50:11,780
And the total duration is the wall clock time that it took.

804
00:50:11,780 --> 00:50:15,940
And then you multiply by the world size,

805
00:50:15,940 --> 00:50:21,340
because it's the total amount that all the ranks have waited.

806
00:50:21,340 --> 00:50:26,620
And the bandwidth is the bytes sent

807
00:50:26,620 --> 00:50:30,340
divided by the total duration.

808
00:50:30,340 --> 00:50:33,460
So in this case, you get something

809
00:50:33,460 --> 00:50:37,520
like about 400 gigabytes per second.

810
00:50:40,620 --> 00:50:45,020
So a few notes here.

811
00:50:45,020 --> 00:50:47,720
One is that the effective bandwidth,

812
00:50:47,720 --> 00:50:50,700
if you look at this expression, size times 2 times

813
00:50:50,700 --> 00:50:53,800
world size minus 1 divided by world size times duration.

814
00:50:53,800 --> 00:50:57,100
So as world size increases, this world size

815
00:50:57,100 --> 00:51:00,320
minus 1 divided by world size essentially converges to 1.

816
00:51:00,320 --> 00:51:03,300
So you're effectively left with 2 times the size

817
00:51:03,300 --> 00:51:05,740
bytes over the duration.

818
00:51:05,740 --> 00:51:08,920
So this is essentially the bandwidth.

819
00:51:08,920 --> 00:51:12,457
Notice that this is independent of the world size,

820
00:51:12,457 --> 00:51:13,040
which is good.

821
00:51:13,040 --> 00:51:16,940
So if you grow the number of GPUs you have,

822
00:51:16,940 --> 00:51:19,140
the bandwidth doesn't change.

823
00:51:19,140 --> 00:51:21,940
It is also independent of the topology, which

824
00:51:21,940 --> 00:51:26,260
is something that kind of NCCL figures out whether you're

825
00:51:26,260 --> 00:51:30,500
going to pass the messages in a kind of a ring or a tree

826
00:51:30,500 --> 00:51:31,360
topology.

827
00:51:34,060 --> 00:51:37,300
So yeah.

828
00:51:37,300 --> 00:51:40,580
So that is all reduce.

829
00:51:40,580 --> 00:51:46,130
And for reduce scatter, this is very similar.

830
00:51:46,130 --> 00:51:48,930
So I'm going to create the inputs and the outputs,

831
00:51:48,930 --> 00:51:54,170
warm up, perform the operation, and time it.

832
00:51:54,170 --> 00:52:02,690
So notice that the reduce scatter has these timings.

833
00:52:02,690 --> 00:52:08,090
And you can also measure the effective bandwidth here,

834
00:52:08,090 --> 00:52:13,330
the number of bytes that were in the input.

835
00:52:13,330 --> 00:52:16,490
You have the number of bytes that were sent.

836
00:52:16,490 --> 00:52:20,850
Here there is no 2x here.

837
00:52:20,850 --> 00:52:23,050
And then the total duration, you divide by that,

838
00:52:23,050 --> 00:52:24,150
you get the bandwidth.

839
00:52:24,150 --> 00:52:29,930
So here, the bandwidth is--it should be very similar.

840
00:52:29,930 --> 00:52:32,090
I guess sometimes there's some stochasticity,

841
00:52:32,090 --> 00:52:36,930
but it's in the kind of 400s.

842
00:52:36,930 --> 00:52:40,090
So a few notes here.

843
00:52:40,090 --> 00:52:44,530
All reduce is, remember, as we stated, reduce

844
00:52:44,530 --> 00:52:47,170
scatter plus an all gather.

845
00:52:47,170 --> 00:52:54,170
And so all reduce, naturally, is moving twice the amount of data,

846
00:52:54,170 --> 00:52:58,890
because reduce scatter has some cost, all gather has some cost,

847
00:52:58,890 --> 00:53:02,970
and all reduce is doing twice as much work.

848
00:53:02,970 --> 00:53:06,810
And it takes twice the amount of time.

849
00:53:06,810 --> 00:53:11,790
But the two cancel out, so you get the same kind of bandwidth.

850
00:53:20,150 --> 00:53:22,690
So that is the end of part one.

851
00:53:22,690 --> 00:53:26,550
Maybe any questions before I move to part two?

852
00:53:30,310 --> 00:53:30,810
Yeah.

853
00:53:30,810 --> 00:53:34,821
Why did you do that synchronize for [INAUDIBLE]?

854
00:53:40,455 --> 00:53:44,050
So why do we have to do the synchronize for the CUDA kernel?

855
00:53:44,050 --> 00:53:49,450
So at the end of the day, we are still doing CUDA operations.

856
00:53:49,450 --> 00:53:53,970
We have just multiple processes, each with a GPU,

857
00:53:53,970 --> 00:53:57,610
doing some CUDA operations.

858
00:53:57,610 --> 00:54:01,150
So if you're doing a CUDA operation, remember, by default,

859
00:54:01,150 --> 00:54:02,730
it's async.

860
00:54:02,730 --> 00:54:05,410
So when you reach the next line in Python,

861
00:54:05,410 --> 00:54:07,690
that CUDA operation might not be done.

862
00:54:07,690 --> 00:54:11,250
So we need to wait always to make sure it's

863
00:54:11,250 --> 00:54:13,650
done by synchronizing.

864
00:54:13,650 --> 00:54:14,850
[INAUDIBLE]

865
00:54:18,210 --> 00:54:25,100
So do you have to do barrier first and then synchronize?

866
00:54:25,100 --> 00:54:27,090
I'm not sure.

867
00:54:27,090 --> 00:54:27,970
Yeah.

868
00:54:27,970 --> 00:54:30,210
[INAUDIBLE]

869
00:54:38,570 --> 00:54:39,070
Yeah.

870
00:54:39,070 --> 00:54:42,200
I think one problem is if you barrier first

871
00:54:42,200 --> 00:54:46,880
and then the CUDA might not be done running,

872
00:54:46,880 --> 00:54:49,440
and you just immediately go to the barrier.

873
00:54:49,440 --> 00:54:54,240
And then you are still each independently synchronizing

874
00:54:54,240 --> 00:54:57,560
the different CUDA kernels, which means that you're not

875
00:54:57,560 --> 00:55:00,580
really synchronized.

876
00:55:00,580 --> 00:55:02,820
If all those operations just return,

877
00:55:02,820 --> 00:55:07,080
the barrier doesn't really do anything.

878
00:55:07,080 --> 00:55:08,560
OK, let me move on.

879
00:55:08,560 --> 00:55:10,880
So now let's actually start thinking

880
00:55:10,880 --> 00:55:12,960
about how you train models.

881
00:55:12,960 --> 00:55:15,280
So we're just going to walk through a very bare bones

882
00:55:15,280 --> 00:55:19,920
implementation of training MLPs, multilayer MLPs.

883
00:55:19,920 --> 00:55:21,140
I guess that's redundant.

884
00:55:21,140 --> 00:55:24,880
It's multilayer perceptrons already.

885
00:55:24,880 --> 00:55:27,680
And remember that MLPs are the ones that

886
00:55:27,680 --> 00:55:29,900
are the actual compute bottleneck in the transformer.

887
00:55:29,900 --> 00:55:33,760
So this is actually pretty representative of what you'll see.

888
00:55:33,760 --> 00:55:37,920
So data parallelism, tensor parallelism,

889
00:55:37,920 --> 00:55:39,640
and pipeline parallelism.

890
00:55:39,640 --> 00:55:44,360
And the picture I want you to have in your head

891
00:55:44,360 --> 00:55:45,780
is this picture.

892
00:55:45,780 --> 00:55:49,260
So this is a little bit of a schematic,

893
00:55:49,260 --> 00:55:51,420
so don't think too deeply about this,

894
00:55:51,420 --> 00:55:55,640
but it's more of a way to conceptualize how you're cutting

895
00:55:55,640 --> 00:55:58,080
your data and parameters.

896
00:55:58,080 --> 00:56:01,560
So data parallelism says, I'm going

897
00:56:01,560 --> 00:56:04,760
to split the data into pieces.

898
00:56:04,760 --> 00:56:08,520
And then each of the GPUs is going

899
00:56:08,520 --> 00:56:10,700
to be responsible for part of the data,

900
00:56:10,700 --> 00:56:14,200
and I'm going to just do normal—

901
00:56:14,200 --> 00:56:16,360
I'm going to keep track of all the parameters

902
00:56:16,360 --> 00:56:20,640
and do normal model training.

903
00:56:20,640 --> 00:56:22,280
And then I need to synchronize.

904
00:56:22,280 --> 00:56:24,900
So let me explain how this works.

905
00:56:24,900 --> 00:56:27,300
So I'm going to generate some sample data.

906
00:56:27,300 --> 00:56:29,740
So there's a batch size of 128.

907
00:56:29,740 --> 00:56:32,480
Number of dimensions, 1,024.

908
00:56:32,480 --> 00:56:38,400
So this is a batch size by num dim matrix, data matrix.

909
00:56:38,400 --> 00:56:44,480
And let's jump into this data parallelism.

910
00:56:44,480 --> 00:56:48,600
So the way that it's going to work

911
00:56:48,600 --> 00:56:51,160
is that you have this data matrix.

912
00:56:51,160 --> 00:57:00,680
And I'm going to break up the rows into world size pieces.

913
00:57:00,680 --> 00:57:02,240
And in this case, four.

914
00:57:02,240 --> 00:57:06,760
And each rank is going to get a piece.

915
00:57:06,760 --> 00:57:09,380
So the number of dimensions, the batch size.

916
00:57:12,120 --> 00:57:18,600
So I'm going to call the local batch size, basically the batch

917
00:57:18,600 --> 00:57:20,360
size divided by the world size, which

918
00:57:20,360 --> 00:57:29,240
is every row that GPU sees, it's going to have 32 data points.

919
00:57:29,240 --> 00:57:30,640
And this is just indexing.

920
00:57:30,640 --> 00:57:37,840
Start index, data start to end gets you the slice of that data.

921
00:57:37,840 --> 00:57:42,720
And I'm going to just put it on that rank.

922
00:57:42,720 --> 00:57:46,720
So at this point, each GPU has now

923
00:57:46,720 --> 00:57:49,840
a distinct data tensor, which is the part

924
00:57:49,840 --> 00:57:52,920
that they're responsible for.

925
00:57:52,920 --> 00:57:55,200
Now, in practice, each rank should probably

926
00:57:55,200 --> 00:57:58,700
load its own data rather than have this bottleneck,

927
00:57:58,700 --> 00:58:01,420
but this is just for illustrative purposes.

928
00:58:04,600 --> 00:58:08,480
So let's instantiate the MLP.

929
00:58:08,480 --> 00:58:15,960
So here, I assume we have layers.

930
00:58:15,960 --> 00:58:19,560
And num layers.

931
00:58:19,560 --> 00:58:23,200
And for each of the layers, I'm going to have just a num

932
00:58:23,200 --> 00:58:27,760
dim by num dim matrix.

933
00:58:27,760 --> 00:58:32,360
So I'm just going to initialize a random set of parameters.

934
00:58:32,360 --> 00:58:36,910
And then I'm going to feed that into optimizer.

935
00:58:36,910 --> 00:58:39,590
So here's the training loop.

936
00:58:39,590 --> 00:58:42,270
So in the forward pass, I take the data.

937
00:58:42,270 --> 00:58:45,810
So remember, data is not all the data.

938
00:58:45,810 --> 00:58:50,990
It's just if I'm rank 2, then I only get B2 part of the data.

939
00:58:50,990 --> 00:58:54,550
I'm going to just go through the number of layers

940
00:58:54,550 --> 00:58:57,430
and do a forward pass.

941
00:58:57,430 --> 00:59:00,470
And then I'm going to do a backward pass.

942
00:59:00,470 --> 00:59:03,590
And then normally, this would be it.

943
00:59:03,590 --> 00:59:09,390
But now, remember, every rank has different data.

944
00:59:09,390 --> 00:59:15,310
Therefore, the gradients are going to be different as well.

945
00:59:15,310 --> 00:59:19,443
So this is the key step that makes data parallelism work.

946
00:59:19,443 --> 00:59:21,110
We're going to synchronize the gradients

947
00:59:21,110 --> 00:59:22,068
across all the workers.

948
00:59:22,068 --> 00:59:24,277
This is the only difference between standard training

949
00:59:24,277 --> 00:59:25,070
and DDP.

950
00:59:25,070 --> 00:59:27,170
It's actually pretty nice and elegant.

951
00:59:27,170 --> 00:59:29,470
So basically, for all the parameters,

952
00:59:29,470 --> 00:59:34,630
I'm going to do an all reduce of param.grad.

953
00:59:37,190 --> 00:59:40,110
And I'm going to average.

954
00:59:40,110 --> 00:59:47,610
然后，all reduce 完成之后，现在在这个阶段，

955
00:59:47,610 --> 00:59:53,030
每个 rank 都有完全相同的梯度。

956
00:59:53,030 --> 00:59:59,350
然后我就直接更新参数。

957
00:59:59,350 --> 01:00:03,990
所以我觉得这真的很优雅，

958
01:00:03,990 --> 01:00:07,590
因为它基本上就是标准训练，你把它应用到

959
01:00:07,590 --> 01:00:11,390
你的本地 batch 上，但只需插入这个

960
01:00:11,390 --> 01:00:12,850
在反向传播之后。

961
01:00:12,850 --> 01:00:16,370
让我们通过这个 all reduce 来平均所有梯度。

962
01:00:16,370 --> 01:00:22,370
只需改一行代码，然后参数就被更新了。

963
01:00:22,370 --> 01:00:27,550
所以在你训练时，每个 rank 基本上

964
01:00:27,550 --> 01:00:32,130
都在进行参数更新，就好像它拥有全部数据一样，

965
01:00:32,130 --> 01:00:36,110
但实际上它只处理了一部分数据。

966
01:00:36,110 --> 01:00:43,750
所以这基本上就是 DDP，或者说第一种数据并行。

967
01:00:43,750 --> 01:00:47,110
关于这个有什么问题吗？

968
01:00:47,110 --> 01:00:48,483
是的。

969
01:00:48,483 --> 01:00:53,670
[INAUDIBLE] 所以问题是，你只能

970
01:00:53,670 --> 01:00:57,450
在 batch size 大于 1 的时候这样做吗？

971
01:00:57,450 --> 01:00:57,950
是的。

972
01:00:57,950 --> 01:01:02,310
所以你的 batch size 至少要等于 world size

973
01:01:02,310 --> 01:01:03,890
才能真正合理。

974
01:01:03,890 --> 01:01:08,670
而且通常，它应该要大得多。

975
01:01:08,670 --> 01:01:09,610
是吗？

976
01:01:09,610 --> 01:01:13,350
[INAUDIBLE] 是的。

977
01:01:13,350 --> 01:01:16,430
所以问题是，batch size 应该是

978
01:01:16,430 --> 01:01:17,850
world size 的倍数吗？

979
01:01:17,850 --> 01:01:20,510
那也会很好。

980
01:01:20,510 --> 01:01:22,070
是的。

981
01:01:22,070 --> 01:01:23,830
我的意思是，如果不是，那你可以用零来填充

982
01:01:23,830 --> 01:01:25,690
或者类似的方法。

983
01:01:25,690 --> 01:01:31,590
所以有办法，但如果它是的话，对每个人来说都更简单。

984
01:01:31,590 --> 01:01:32,710
[INAUDIBLE]？

985
01:01:39,870 --> 01:01:44,710
对于基于 transformer 的 [INAUDIBLE] 会是什么样子？

986
01:01:44,710 --> 01:01:46,670
是的，所以问题是，这会是什么样子

987
01:01:46,670 --> 01:01:47,890
对于 transformer 来说？

988
01:01:47,890 --> 01:01:50,990
实际上基本是一样的。

989
01:01:50,990 --> 01:01:53,910
DDP 有一个很好的特点，就是非常模块化。

990
01:01:53,910 --> 01:01:55,910
你做前向传播。

991
01:01:55,910 --> 01:01:59,250
我的意思是，DDP 只是在这里平均参数。

992
01:01:59,250 --> 01:02:01,810
它不关心你的前向传播是什么样子。

993
01:02:04,470 --> 01:02:08,130
好的，让我继续。

994
01:02:08,130 --> 01:02:11,470
So that's DDP.

995
01:02:11,470 --> 01:02:18,510
So just to summarize, the losses are different across the ranks.

996
01:02:18,510 --> 01:02:22,870
The gradients are also initially different.

997
01:02:22,870 --> 01:02:26,150
But they are all reduced to be the same across the ranks.

998
01:02:26,150 --> 01:02:27,830
And therefore, the parameters all

999
01:02:27,830 --> 01:02:32,500
remain the same across ranks.

1000
01:02:32,500 --> 01:02:34,660
So next lecture, Tatsu is going

1001
01:02:34,660 --> 01:02:39,580
to talk about fancier data parallelism, FSDP and ZeRO.

1002
01:02:39,580 --> 01:02:42,940
And the idea there is, as I've alluded to,

1003
01:02:42,940 --> 01:02:44,560
here we use all reduce.

1004
01:02:44,560 --> 01:02:47,100
It's a very simple monolithic operation,

1005
01:02:47,100 --> 01:02:50,140
but it does require holding all the models, parameters

1006
01:02:50,140 --> 01:02:51,380
in memory.

1007
01:02:51,380 --> 01:02:54,140
But what if the model parameters don't fit in memory?

1008
01:02:54,140 --> 01:02:56,000
Then you're going to have to be more clever,

1009
01:02:56,000 --> 01:02:59,140
and that's the topic for the next class.

1010
01:02:59,140 --> 01:03:03,980
So let me talk about tensor parallelism.

1011
01:03:03,980 --> 01:03:07,700
So here, the idea is we're going to cut this way

1012
01:03:07,700 --> 01:03:09,200
and we're not going to cut the data.

1013
01:03:09,200 --> 01:03:16,140
We're going to cut essentially each layer.

1014
01:03:16,140 --> 01:03:22,660
And so each rank is going to get part of each layer.

1015
01:03:22,660 --> 01:03:26,260
And generally, this means that we're

1016
01:03:26,260 --> 01:03:29,080
going to have to transfer a lot more data.

1017
01:03:29,080 --> 01:03:30,840
We'll discuss this a bit later.

1018
01:03:33,820 --> 01:03:37,740
So what does tensor parallel look like?

1019
01:03:37,740 --> 01:03:42,840
So we're just going to assume we have this data.

1020
01:03:42,840 --> 01:03:47,980
Every rank has all the data just for simplicity.

1021
01:03:47,980 --> 01:03:54,300
And here, remember, the data is batch size times num dim.

1022
01:03:54,300 --> 01:04:00,660
And I'm going to define a local num dim to be-- for this rank, I only

1023
01:04:00,660 --> 01:04:07,140
am responsible for a subset of the dimensions.

1024
01:04:07,140 --> 01:04:15,180
So the picture here is that each model still has all the layers

1025
01:04:15,180 --> 01:04:18,500
here for all the layers, but the parameters now

1026
01:04:18,500 --> 01:04:24,200
are num dim times local num dim.

1027
01:04:24,200 --> 01:04:26,460
So if this were one of the parameter

1028
01:04:26,460 --> 01:04:35,020
matrices for one layer, I would be splitting down the columns.

1029
01:04:35,020 --> 01:04:38,940
So this is also known as column tensor parallel.

1030
01:04:38,940 --> 01:04:40,900
You can also do it by rows, but we're not going

1031
01:04:40,900 --> 01:04:43,860
to talk about that right now.

1032
01:04:43,860 --> 01:04:48,300
So now what does the forward pass look like?

1033
01:04:48,300 --> 01:04:50,340
So I'll go through all the layers

1034
01:04:50,340 --> 01:04:53,340
And I'm going to compute activations.

1035
01:04:53,340 --> 01:05:00,700
So I start with x, the data.

1036
01:05:00,700 --> 01:05:07,580
And I'm going to access the parameters at layer.

1037
01:05:07,580 --> 01:05:11,040
And notice that this is only a slice of the parameters.

1038
01:05:11,040 --> 01:05:16,580
So if I'm on rank 1, I only get this part of the matrix.

1039
01:05:16,580 --> 01:05:18,200
But I can still proceed.

1040
01:05:18,200 --> 01:05:23,700
I can apply this non-linearity, because this is element-wise anyway.

1041
01:05:23,700 --> 01:05:27,770
But now what I'm going to do is I'm going

1042
01:05:27,770 --> 01:05:32,290
to communicate activations.

1043
01:05:32,290 --> 01:05:35,250
So if I have a data matrix, rank 1

1044
01:05:35,250 --> 01:05:38,570
has activations for part of the activations

1045
01:05:38,570 --> 01:05:41,210
for this part of the matrix.

1046
01:05:41,210 --> 01:05:43,930
Rank 1 has part of the activations for this matrix

1047
01:05:43,930 --> 01:05:45,290
and so on and so forth.

1048
01:05:45,290 --> 01:05:49,330
And I need to basically put all the activations

1049
01:05:49,330 --> 01:05:52,650
on all the ranks.

1050
01:05:52,650 --> 01:05:54,470
But we know how to do that.

1051
01:05:54,470 --> 01:05:58,990
We introduced all gather as a collective primitive.

1052
01:05:58,990 --> 01:06:05,050
So this is all the activations.

1053
01:06:05,050 --> 01:06:10,970
So this is the batch size times local num dim, which is

1054
01:06:10,970 --> 01:06:15,290
the shape of the activations.

1055
01:06:15,290 --> 01:06:20,890
And then I'm doing an all gather.

1056
01:06:20,890 --> 01:06:23,930
Sorry, so this is the allocating memory for the activations.

1057
01:06:23,930 --> 01:06:27,870
So x is the actual part of the activation.

1058
01:06:27,870 --> 01:06:31,050
So this is batch size times local num dim.

1059
01:06:31,050 --> 01:06:37,530
And all gather says each rank has x and each rank

1060
01:06:37,530 --> 01:06:41,810
is going to allocate activations,

1061
01:06:41,810 --> 01:06:44,410
which is a list, one for each world size.

1062
01:06:44,410 --> 01:06:46,610
And then after the all gather, x is

1063
01:06:46,610 --> 01:06:50,930
going to be copied into each of the respective location

1064
01:06:50,930 --> 01:06:54,150
activations.

1065
01:06:54,150 --> 01:06:57,310
So then once I gather all the activations,

1066
01:06:57,310 --> 01:07:00,890
I concatenate them to form the full dimensional x, which

1067
01:07:00,890 --> 01:07:02,590
is batch size times num dim.

1068
01:07:08,530 --> 01:07:14,310
Any questions about column tensor parallel?

1069
01:07:18,130 --> 01:07:21,530
So this is done for every layer.

1070
01:07:21,530 --> 01:07:26,450
We now notice one difference between data parallel

1071
01:07:26,450 --> 01:07:29,450
is now we have to muck around with the model.

1072
01:07:29,450 --> 01:07:32,230
Data parallel is very elegant, because it's splitting by data.

1073
01:07:32,230 --> 01:07:35,490
The model is treated as a module.

1074
01:07:35,490 --> 01:07:38,890
But now we have to muck around with the model.

1075
01:07:38,890 --> 01:07:42,290
And this is strongly leveraging the fact

1076
01:07:42,290 --> 01:07:45,730
that if you want to do a matrix multiplication,

1077
01:07:45,730 --> 01:07:50,050
you can split it up into a set of smaller matrix

1078
01:07:50,050 --> 01:07:51,150
multiplications.

1079
01:07:51,150 --> 01:07:52,770
We can do those on different ranks,

1080
01:07:52,770 --> 01:07:55,310
and then we can gather the results.

1081
01:07:58,290 --> 01:08:00,050
When it comes to the backpropagation,

1082
01:08:00,050 --> 01:08:08,010
gradients are still [INAUDIBLE].

1083
01:08:08,650 --> 01:08:12,850
So now the question is, what happens in backprop?

1084
01:08:12,850 --> 01:08:16,490
Now, in the backprop, you have your activations

1085
01:08:16,490 --> 01:08:22,870
and you have to reduce scatter to all the different gradients.

1086
01:08:22,870 --> 01:08:25,670
So in some ways, all gather and reduce scatter

1087
01:08:25,670 --> 01:08:28,290
have this kind of duality, where in forward, you're

1088
01:08:28,290 --> 01:08:33,490
all gathering, in the backward, you're reduce scattering.

1089
01:08:33,490 --> 01:08:34,430
Yeah?

1090
01:08:34,430 --> 01:08:36,850
[INAUDIBLE]

1091
01:08:36,850 --> 01:08:39,950
So the question is, is that done automatically by autograd?

1092
01:08:39,950 --> 01:08:43,490
So none of this--well, OK.

1093
01:08:43,490 --> 01:08:46,689
So if you just call .backward, it's not going to do it,

1094
01:08:46,689 --> 01:08:49,630
because there's no parallelism in that.

1095
01:08:49,630 --> 01:08:53,130
But PyTorch has all these things that

1096
01:08:53,130 --> 01:08:54,990
are done automatically for you.

1097
01:08:58,490 --> 01:09:05,850
[INAUDIBLE] To what extent do I need to change

1098
01:09:05,850 --> 01:09:09,290
my code and [INAUDIBLE]?

1099
01:09:09,290 --> 01:09:14,729
So what is done for you versus automatically?

1100
01:09:14,729 --> 01:09:18,880
Here we're managing things fairly explicitly,

1101
01:09:18,880 --> 01:09:21,740
which means I'm not doing the backward pass,

1102
01:09:21,740 --> 01:09:25,200
but you would have to manage and call the reduce scatter

1103
01:09:25,200 --> 01:09:25,939
yourself.

1104
01:09:30,439 --> 01:09:33,997
And that's by design, because this is 336,

1105
01:09:33,997 --> 01:09:35,580
building language models from scratch.

1106
01:09:35,580 --> 01:09:38,840
In practice, you probably wouldn't have to do that.

1107
01:09:38,840 --> 01:09:42,359
OK, so let's do pipeline parallelism quickly.

1108
01:09:42,359 --> 01:09:44,920
So the idea behind pipeline parallelism

1109
01:09:44,920 --> 01:09:47,760
is we're going to split the network this way.

1110
01:09:47,760 --> 01:09:52,800
So each rank is going to get a subset of the layers now.

1111
01:09:52,800 --> 01:09:56,360
Within each layer, it's going to get all the dimensions,

1112
01:09:56,360 --> 01:09:59,200
and it's also going to get all the--

1113
01:09:59,200 --> 01:10:04,080
Well, one of the ranks is going to get all the data.

1114
01:10:04,080 --> 01:10:08,640
Every rank is going to see all the data in some form.

1115
01:10:08,640 --> 01:10:14,180
So this is the way it's going to work.

1116
01:10:14,180 --> 01:10:20,100
So we have all the data, which is, again,

1117
01:10:20,100 --> 01:10:23,320
the batch size times num dim.

1118
01:10:23,320 --> 01:10:26,360
And I'm going to split up the layers.

1119
01:10:26,360 --> 01:10:29,040
So local num layers is going to be the number of layers

1120
01:10:29,040 --> 01:10:32,920
that a particular rank is going to handle.

1121
01:10:32,920 --> 01:10:37,520
And so now I'm going to have local params, which

1122
01:10:37,520 --> 01:10:40,920
is basically only-- there's local number of layers

1123
01:10:40,920 --> 01:10:42,320
number of them.

1124
01:10:42,320 --> 01:10:48,040
But within each layer, I'm going to do the num dim by num dim.

1125
01:10:48,040 --> 01:10:53,760
So one thing, maybe [INAUDIBLE] will talk more

1126
01:10:53,760 --> 01:10:59,520
about this on Wednesday, is this idea of micro-batches.

1127
01:10:59,520 --> 01:11:02,680
So maybe I'll just present this and explain

1128
01:11:02,680 --> 01:11:04,960
why I'm doing things this way.

1129
01:11:04,960 --> 01:11:07,780
So in addition to splitting up the layers,

1130
01:11:07,780 --> 01:11:10,280
I'm also going to split up the batch

1131
01:11:10,280 --> 01:11:14,400
into a bunch of micro-batches.

1132
01:11:14,400 --> 01:11:20,640
So if I'm rank 0, then I get the data,

1133
01:11:20,640 --> 01:11:24,680
and I'm going to chunk it up into a number of micro-batches.

1134
01:11:24,680 --> 01:11:32,240
And for each micro-batch, what I'm going to do

1135
01:11:32,240 --> 01:11:39,440
is receive it from the previous rank,

1136
01:11:39,440 --> 01:11:43,840
and then do the feed forward pass only on the layers that

1137
01:11:43,840 --> 01:11:45,480
are assigned to this rank.

1138
01:11:45,480 --> 01:11:51,760
And then I'm going to send to the next rank.

1139
01:11:51,760 --> 01:11:55,240
So here I'm actually using these receive and send,

1140
01:11:55,240 --> 01:11:57,240
which are pointwise operations.

1141
01:11:57,240 --> 01:12:02,220
So I didn't cover those before, but they're fairly explanatory.

1142
01:12:02,220 --> 01:12:06,200
This basically says I'm rank and I'm going

1143
01:12:06,200 --> 01:12:09,280
to send this tensor over to--

1144
01:12:09,280 --> 01:12:14,230
sorry, I'm going to receive this tensor from rank minus 1.

1145
01:12:14,230 --> 01:12:17,770
And this says I'm going to send tensor x to rank plus 1.

1146
01:12:28,750 --> 01:12:35,710
So basically, the reason why I'm talking about micro-batches is--

1147
01:12:35,710 --> 01:12:38,510
and [? Tatsu ?] will talk more about this on Wednesday--

1148
01:12:38,510 --> 01:12:41,990
is that in pipeline parallelism, so you have

1149
01:12:41,990 --> 01:12:46,030
one rank that gets the data.

1150
01:12:46,030 --> 01:12:48,670
It processes some of the layers, and then

1151
01:12:48,670 --> 01:12:53,930
it sends it to the next GPU, and it processes some of the layers.

1152
01:12:53,930 --> 01:12:56,710
And it then sends it to the next GPU.

1153
01:12:56,710 --> 01:13:00,770
So this is a very natural way of dividing a deep network.

1154
01:13:00,770 --> 01:13:03,070
But the problem is that you get these

1155
01:13:03,070 --> 01:13:09,430
what are called pipeline bubbles, where while you're not

1156
01:13:09,430 --> 01:13:13,190
processing, you're kind of waiting around for other tensors

1157
01:13:13,190 --> 01:13:14,630
to process.

1158
01:13:14,630 --> 01:13:18,310
And this ends up being quite inefficient.

1159
01:13:18,310 --> 01:13:22,710
So the idea behind micro-batches is that you break it up

1160
01:13:22,710 --> 01:13:25,430
into smaller batches, so you can process it quickly,

1161
01:13:25,430 --> 01:13:26,850
send it on to the next one.

1162
01:13:26,850 --> 01:13:32,470
So this can reduce the number of pipeline bubbles.

1163
01:13:32,470 --> 01:13:34,390
So the other thing I will mention

1164
01:13:34,390 --> 01:13:37,630
that is not handled in this very kind of naive version

1165
01:13:37,630 --> 01:13:41,410
is the idea of overlapping communication and computation,

1166
01:13:41,410 --> 01:13:45,210
which is actually very important to pipeline parallelism.

1167
01:13:45,210 --> 01:13:47,390
This basically gives you the right structure.

1168
01:13:47,390 --> 01:13:53,550
If you put an I before these, then it becomes kind of async.

1169
01:13:53,550 --> 01:13:58,510
You have to add more things to manage the code.

1170
01:13:58,510 --> 01:14:02,750
And the idea here is that you want to be--

1171
01:14:02,750 --> 01:14:07,510
while you're computing here, you can be

1172
01:14:07,510 --> 01:14:11,270
receiving data or sending data.

1173
01:14:11,270 --> 01:14:14,490
So computation and communication should overlap.

1174
01:14:14,490 --> 01:14:19,210
So that reduces the amount of time you actually spend waiting.

1175
01:14:23,190 --> 01:14:30,110
So a few things that are kind of missing here, which we'll

1176
01:14:30,110 --> 01:14:32,110
hopefully fill in next time.

1177
01:14:32,110 --> 01:14:36,190
So the communication versus computation overlap,

1178
01:14:36,190 --> 01:14:41,950
which is especially crucial in pipeline parallelism.

1179
01:14:41,950 --> 01:14:44,370
I didn't mention in data parallelism,

1180
01:14:44,370 --> 01:14:48,810
this also happens, because I just did a forward pass

1181
01:14:48,810 --> 01:14:51,650
and then at the end, I'm just doing all these all reduces.

1182
01:14:51,650 --> 01:14:55,110
But if you're clever, then on the backward pass,

1183
01:14:55,110 --> 01:14:57,090
as soon as the gradients are done,

1184
01:14:57,090 --> 01:14:58,775
you can start sending that.

1185
01:14:58,775 --> 01:15:00,150
And that's something that will be

1186
01:15:00,150 --> 01:15:02,510
explored in the assignment two.

1187
01:15:02,510 --> 01:15:06,910
And this, again, allows you to just overlap communication

1188
01:15:06,910 --> 01:15:10,630
and computation more.

1189
01:15:10,630 --> 01:15:14,410
We had some questions about what about general models.

1190
01:15:14,410 --> 01:15:19,270
Again, I think this MLP gives you

1191
01:15:19,270 --> 01:15:24,310
essentially most of what you need

1192
01:15:24,310 --> 01:15:27,750
for understanding the basics.

1193
01:15:27,750 --> 01:15:30,970
有些更大的模型需要更多的簿记工作，

1194
01:15:30,970 --> 01:15:36,750
所以更难看到核心算法。

1195
01:15:36,750 --> 01:15:39,950
还有一些我们没有讲到的其他类型的并行计算。

1196
01:15:39,950 --> 01:15:43,230
序列并行把一整个序列

1197
01:15:43,230 --> 01:15:44,990
切成若干块。

1198
01:15:44,990 --> 01:15:47,110
这样就可以并行化注意力

1199
01:15:47,110 --> 01:15:52,670
计算，专家并行则可以并行化

1200
01:15:52,670 --> 01:15:54,250
MoE 中的专家。

1201
01:15:54,250 --> 01:15:57,590
这就是我提到的 all-to-all 发挥作用的地方。

1202
01:15:57,590 --> 01:16:02,350
然后还有不同的组合

1203
01:16:02,350 --> 01:16:06,070
不同的并行化技术，这也会

1204
01:16:06,070 --> 01:16:10,140
出现在作业里。

1205
01:16:10,140 --> 01:16:14,460
需要注意的一点是，

1206
01:16:14,460 --> 01:16:17,580
你选择哪种并行技术

1207
01:16:17,580 --> 01:16:21,500
很大程度上取决于硬件。

1208
01:16:21,500 --> 01:16:23,660
例如，张量并行。

1209
01:16:23,660 --> 01:16:27,460
通信量很大，因为每一层

1210
01:16:27,460 --> 01:16:31,860
都需要发送所有这些激活值，而它们相当大。

1211
01:16:31,860 --> 01:16:36,220
所以通常张量并行发生在节点内，通过 NVLink

1212
01:16:36,220 --> 01:16:40,740
或者在高带宽的情况下。

1213
01:16:40,740 --> 01:16:42,740
而你不会跨 NVLink 域

1214
01:16:42,740 --> 01:16:46,820
做张量并行。

1215
01:16:46,820 --> 01:16:50,800
而流水线并行，你会看到有人在用。

1216
01:16:50,800 --> 01:16:58,300
通常它可以容忍慢得多的互连。

1217
01:16:58,300 --> 01:17:02,020
所以一些去中心化训练的工作

1218
01:17:02,020 --> 01:17:05,580
使用流水线并行，因为你的节点——

1219
01:17:05,580 --> 01:17:09,340
GPU 分布在世界各地。

1220
01:17:09,340 --> 01:17:14,220
但在那种情况下你不想做张量并行。

1221
01:17:14,220 --> 01:17:18,220
所以有时候你看这些组合，

1222
01:17:18,220 --> 01:17:21,100
会是节点内张量并行，

1223
01:17:21,100 --> 01:17:28,880
然后是流水线数据并行或 FSDP，

1224
01:17:28,880 --> 01:17:35,420
然后如果需要的话再用流水线并行。

1225
01:17:35,420 --> 01:17:39,460
还有其他影响，比如如果你做数据并行，

1226
01:17:39,460 --> 01:17:42,600
你可能可以把数据并行做得比较大。

1227
01:17:42,600 --> 01:17:45,060
但随后你会遇到一个

1228
01:17:45,060 --> 01:17:47,340
叫做临界批量大小的东西，这时

1229
01:17:47,340 --> 01:17:49,960
如果你把批量大小增加得太多，

1230
01:17:49,960 --> 01:17:52,100
它实际上并不会帮到你，这种情况下

1231
01:17:52,100 --> 01:17:53,740
你就是在浪费你的算力，

1232
01:17:53,740 --> 01:17:56,160
那你不如使用张量并行。

1233
01:17:56,160 --> 01:17:58,020
有很多这样的考虑因素，

1234
01:17:58,020 --> 01:18:03,340
随着课程的推进，我们会进一步讨论这些。

1235
01:18:03,340 --> 01:18:04,700
最后再说明一点。

1236
01:18:04,700 --> 01:18:08,060
刚才简单提到了TPU。

1237
01:18:08,060 --> 01:18:14,700
需要注意的是，我们有意使用PyTorch，

1238
01:18:14,700 --> 01:18:17,220
而且不只是使用PyTorch，而是真正以

1239
01:18:17,220 --> 01:18:21,780
非常基础的方式使用集体操作，

1240
01:18:21,780 --> 01:18:25,660
这样你就能从机制上看到发生了什么。

1241
01:18:25,660 --> 01:18:29,700
另一种方法，尤其是如果你在TPU领域，

1242
01:18:29,700 --> 01:18:34,540
就是你可以直接定义模型和起始

1243
01:18:34,540 --> 01:18:35,740
策略。

1244
01:18:35,740 --> 01:18:38,380
编译器实际上会处理很多

1245
01:18:38,380 --> 01:18:44,060
关于如何——

1246
01:18:44,060 --> 01:18:48,400
基本上，你需要什么样的通信操作。

1247
01:18:48,400 --> 01:18:51,180
你基本上是说，这块数据

1248
01:18:51,180 --> 01:18:53,760
需要在这里、这里和这里。

1249
01:18:53,760 --> 01:18:58,940
然后编译器会做一些神奇的事情来弄清楚。

1250
01:18:58,940 --> 01:19:00,120
所以这很有吸引力。

1251
01:19:00,120 --> 01:19:04,460
但显然，这会剥夺很多

1252
01:19:04,460 --> 01:19:09,020
从零开始构建东西的乐趣。

1253
01:19:09,020 --> 01:19:14,660
总结一下，有很多并行化的方式。

1254
01:19:14,660 --> 01:19:19,860
你可以按数据切分，按张量或专家切分，

1255
01:19:19,860 --> 01:19:23,700
按流水线或序列切分。

1256
01:19:23,700 --> 01:19:26,460
我们看了数据并行。

1257
01:19:26,460 --> 01:19:28,180
我们只做了DDP。

1258
01:19:28,180 --> 01:19:32,140
下次我们会讲FSDP和0。

1259
01:19:32,140 --> 01:19:34,460
张量并行，正如我提到的，

1260
01:19:34,460 --> 01:19:36,820
需要非常快的互连。

1261
01:19:36,820 --> 01:19:38,980
流水线并行则不那么需要。

1262
01:19:38,980 --> 01:19:42,140
但你需要非常努力地减少这些流水线

1263
01:19:42,140 --> 01:19:44,300
气泡。

1264
01:19:44,300 --> 01:19:47,060
然后也许在高层面上，我们

1265
01:19:47,060 --> 01:19:50,900
看到这个模式经常出现。

1266
01:19:50,900 --> 01:19:56,485
所以你可以选择重新计算或存储在内存中，

1267
01:19:56,485 --> 01:19:57,860
当我们讨论像

1268
01:19:57,860 --> 01:20:04,370
激活检查点或使用GPU时。

1269
01:20:04,370 --> 01:20:07,410
或者在这种情况下，你可以把这看作一种扩展，

1270
01:20:07,410 --> 01:20:12,130
即你可以存储在不同的GPU上。

1271
01:20:12,130 --> 01:20:15,290
从这个角度来看，你看数据并行。

1272
01:20:15,290 --> 01:20:17,810
在某种意义上，你在做冗余工作，

1273
01:20:17,810 --> 01:20:23,010
because every rank is actually updating its parameters

1274
01:20:23,010 --> 01:20:25,130
and keeping track of all the parameters.

1275
01:20:25,130 --> 01:20:26,890
But the reason you're doing that is

1276
01:20:26,890 --> 01:20:33,090
that you don't have to move the optimizer state across.

1277
01:20:33,090 --> 01:20:37,230
So one thing is that hardware is getting faster.

1278
01:20:37,230 --> 01:20:40,910
But in some sense, we'll always want bigger models.

1279
01:20:40,910 --> 01:20:44,250
So this idea of having a hierarchical structure

1280
01:20:44,250 --> 01:20:47,210
will always be there.

1281
01:20:47,210 --> 01:20:49,910
So that's it for today.

1282
01:20:49,910 --> 01:20:54,370
So next Wednesday, Tatsu will do more of a deep dive

1283
01:20:54,370 --> 01:20:57,680
on more parallelism techniques.
